{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-336603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{-7/10}^{-1/5}\\sqrt{10x+7}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =10x+7 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =10 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=10x+7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -\\frac{7}{10} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ u & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7/10}^{-1/5}\\sqrt{10x+7}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}({5}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}(({5}^{3})^{1/2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-336603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-318994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}\\cdot {({x}^{3}+5)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-318994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 5 & 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}\\cdot {({x}^{3}+5)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{5}^{6}{u}^{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{6}2{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =2{\\int }_{5}^{6}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({6}^{4}-{5}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1296-625) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 671 \\\\  & =\\frac{671}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-318994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-318994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-318994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{671}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-17103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{({x}^{3}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-17103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{({x}^{3}+1)}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({9}^{5/2}-{1}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(({3}^{2})^{5/2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({3}^{5}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{484}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-17103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-17103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-17103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{448}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{484}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{48}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{844}"] ["math" "\\frac{844}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-17103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{484}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{3}\\cdot {({x}^{4}+1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{4}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{3}\\cdot {({x}^{4}+1)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-336612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{14}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2x\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({4}^{5/2}-{1}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(({2}^{2})^{5/2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({2}^{5}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{62}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-336803" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336803" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{62}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-13189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the definite integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-13189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3x+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3x+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{5}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-13189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-13189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-13189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-13189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-19129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-19129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =1+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\,\\text{d}u & =4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})}\\cdot 4x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u & =2{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-19129" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-19129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-19129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-19129" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{4}+9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}+9.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 9 & 25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{({x}^{4}+9)}\\,{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{9}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-73432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{5{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5{x}^{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =20{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =20{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5{x}^{4}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{(5{x}^{4}+4)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-336729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-336729" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ln(3)-\\ln(2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{({x}^{3}+2)}\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1307ea80fa1555340e9ce70af1f9ed7e9f363a1f9431beca0a67434869cf4048"} {"question" "q-73434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "q-73434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/state.json" "file_sha256" "31fcb4c8d8ed49b0b76d569ffb3fdb8b03a64b8bc73e5706135920af3a7227a4"} {"question" "e-455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/example-455.json" "file_sha256" "0ce7fb51ccc601b7bc8a5586ccd43e5b7a5c40c6f279fd91248d7f943e30b078"} {"question" "e-455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^2+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}{|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}-{2}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-16}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u.$ When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x.$ Same for the upper limit." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/example-455.json" "file_sha256" "0ce7fb51ccc601b7bc8a5586ccd43e5b7a5c40c6f279fd91248d7f943e30b078"} {"question" "e-456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/example-456.json" "file_sha256" "a9bb0b5636dde257c28a1ca01dbc82c9f17aa6fb7881eec98953a1e262af934c"} {"question" "e-456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^3+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004889/example-456.json" "file_sha256" "a9bb0b5636dde257c28a1ca01dbc82c9f17aa6fb7881eec98953a1e262af934c"}] "usage" {"e-456" [] "e-455" []} "basis" 1100 "retractions" [[17592186100617 :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186100617 :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{2} + 1 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\ndu &= 2xdx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}du &= xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^2+1\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{u^{4}}{4}\\right|_{2}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1^{4} - 2^{4}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 16}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u$. When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x$. Same for the upper limit."] [17592186100618 :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186100618 :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{3} + 2 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\ndu &= 3x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}du &= x^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^3+2\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{3}\\ln (u)\\right|_{1}^{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln (10) - \\ln (1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (10)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
